"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,610\nThe Learning Brain. Series 8: Assessment That Teaches\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,340\nEpisode 4\n\n3\n00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:09,530\nTest before you teach\n\n4\n00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:14,240\nHere is a strange idea.\n\n5\n00:00:14,595 --> 00:00:17,322\nTest students on material before you teach it,\n\n6\n00:00:17,452 --> 00:00:19,757\nwhen they will mostly get the answers wrong.\n\n7\n00:00:20,157 --> 00:00:21,593\nCould that help them learn?\n\n8\n00:00:21,763 --> 00:00:23,180\nIn 2009, Lindsey Richland,\n\n9\n00:00:23,431 --> 00:00:30,856\nNate Kornell and Liche Sean Kao gave students questions about a science passage before they read it.\n\n10\n00:00:31,409 --> 00:00:34,794\nStudents got almost all of the pretest questions wrong.\n\n11\n00:00:34,964 --> 00:00:42,036\nBut after reading, they remembered the pretested information better than students who simply read the passage for longer.\n\n12\n00:00:42,509 --> 00:00:46,986\nAnd the benefit held even for the questions they had answered incorrectly.\n\n13\n00:00:47,156 --> 00:00:54,400\nA 2023 review by Steven Pan and Shana Carpenter found the pretesting effect across texts,\n\n14\n00:00:54,622 --> 00:01:02,153\nvideos and lectures, with one key condition: students need a chance to study the correct answers afterwards.\n\n15\n00:01:02,323 --> 00:01:03,668\nWhy would a wrong guess help?\n\n16\n00:01:04,154 --> 00:01:06,551\nA pretest tells students what matters,\n\n17\n00:01:06,736 --> 00:01:08,851\nso they pay attention when it comes up.\n\n18\n00:01:09,209 --> 00:01:11,918\nAnd searching memory, even unsuccessfully,\n\n19\n00:01:12,138 --> 00:01:15,569\nprimes the connections that the right answer will attach to.\n\n20\n00:01:15,739 --> 00:01:18,596\nIt also changes how students see errors.\n\n21\n00:01:18,992 --> 00:01:21,476\nA mistake on a pretest costs nothing,\n\n22\n00:01:21,651 --> 00:01:23,425\nand becomes the start of learning,\n\n23\n00:01:23,538 --> 00:01:24,636\nnot a judgment.\n\n24\n00:01:24,806 --> 00:01:25,778\nSome caution.\n\n25\n00:01:26,158 --> 00:01:27,683\nPretests should be ungraded,\n\n26\n00:01:27,703 --> 00:01:30,481\nand followed by the material that answers them.\n\n27\n00:01:30,951 --> 00:01:33,563\nStudents also tend to dislike guessing,\n\n28\n00:01:33,774 --> 00:01:35,991\nso explain why you are doing it.\n\n29\n00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:40,056\nAnd the clearest benefit is for the pretested information itself,\n\n30\n00:01:40,303 --> 00:01:43,171\nso ask about the ideas that matter most.\n\n31\n00:01:43,341 --> 00:01:44,136\nFor teaching.\n\n32\n00:01:44,476 --> 00:01:48,951\nStart a unit or a video with two or three questions students can't yet answer.\n\n33\n00:01:49,304 --> 00:01:50,258\nDon't grade them.\n\n34\n00:01:50,546 --> 00:01:53,297\nThen teach, and return to the questions at the end.\n\n35\n00:01:53,742 --> 00:01:55,666\nOne tip to share with students: guess first,\n\n36\n00:01:55,891 --> 00:01:58,071\nthen check the answer.\n\n37\n00:01:58,241 --> 00:02:00,845\nA wrong answer, followed by the right one,\n\n38\n00:02:01,068 --> 00:02:02,534\ncan be the start of learning.\n\n"